WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.002
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online B one, Book three.

2
00:00:05.302 --> 00:00:08.781
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.081 --> 00:00:15.727
Unit three: Extended Family. Today you will talk about long traditions with the present perfect continuous.

4
00:00:16.027 --> 00:00:18.877
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.833 --> 00:00:23.182
Part one: words. You will learn twelve new words.

6
00:00:24.017 --> 00:00:24.925
In-law.

7
00:00:25.917 --> 00:00:28.172
In-law means a relative through marriage.

8
00:00:28.783 --> 00:00:31.050
My sister's in-laws live in Battambang.

9
00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:33.977
Your turn. Say: In-law.

10
00:00:36.833 --> 00:00:37.846
In-law.

11
00:00:38.583 --> 00:00:39.468
Reunion.

12
00:00:40.483 --> 00:00:44.027
Reunion means a meeting of people after a long time apart.

13
00:00:44.617 --> 00:00:47.313
We have a family reunion every Khmer New Year.

14
00:00:47.917 --> 00:00:50.592
Your turn. Say: Reunion.

15
00:00:53.433 --> 00:00:54.342
Reunion.

16
00:00:55.083 --> 00:00:55.992
Heritage.

17
00:00:56.983 --> 00:00:59.819
Heritage means the culture and history passed to you.

18
00:01:00.417 --> 00:01:02.428
We are proud of our Khmer heritage.

19
00:01:03.017 --> 00:01:05.367
Your turn. Say: Heritage.

20
00:01:08.233 --> 00:01:09.327
Heritage.

21
00:01:10.083 --> 00:01:11.340
Ancestral home.

22
00:01:12.350 --> 00:01:15.801
Ancestral home means the house where your family lived long ago.

23
00:01:16.417 --> 00:01:18.451
Our ancestral home is in Takeo.

24
00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:21.760
Your turn. Say: Ancestral home.

25
00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:26.065
Ancestral home.

26
00:01:26.817 --> 00:01:28.178
Generation gap.

27
00:01:29.183 --> 00:01:32.669
Generation gap means differences between older and younger people.

28
00:01:33.283 --> 00:01:35.132
Phones show the generation gap.

29
00:01:35.717 --> 00:01:38.636
Your turn. Say: Generation gap.

30
00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:42.873
Generation gap.

31
00:01:43.617 --> 00:01:44.920
Family tradition.

32
00:01:45.917 --> 00:01:49.379
Family tradition means something a family does every year.

33
00:01:49.983 --> 00:01:52.436
Making num ansom is our family tradition.

34
00:01:53.050 --> 00:01:56.120
Your turn. Say: Family tradition.

35
00:01:58.967 --> 00:02:00.177
Family tradition.

36
00:02:00.917 --> 00:02:01.639
Bond.

37
00:02:02.650 --> 00:02:05.427
Bond means a strong feeling that connects people.

38
00:02:06.017 --> 00:02:07.981
I have a close bond with my cousins.

39
00:02:08.583 --> 00:02:10.899
Your turn. Say: Bond.

40
00:02:13.733 --> 00:02:14.525
Bond.

41
00:02:15.283 --> 00:02:16.784
Respect elders.

42
00:02:17.783 --> 00:02:20.863
Respect elders means to show respect to older people.

43
00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:23.856
In Cambodia, we respect elders.

44
00:02:24.450 --> 00:02:27.985
Your turn. Say: Respect elders.

45
00:02:30.833 --> 00:02:32.264
Respect elders.

46
00:02:33.017 --> 00:02:33.855
Gathering.

47
00:02:34.850 --> 00:02:37.105
Gathering means a meeting of many people.

48
00:02:37.717 --> 00:02:39.867
The family gathering was full of food.

49
00:02:40.483 --> 00:02:42.903
Your turn. Say: Gathering.

50
00:02:45.767 --> 00:02:46.559
Gathering.

51
00:02:47.317 --> 00:02:48.457
Legacy.

52
00:02:49.450 --> 00:02:52.843
Legacy means something left for future generations.

53
00:02:53.450 --> 00:02:55.914
My grandfather's legacy is our rice farm.

54
00:02:56.517 --> 00:02:58.763
Your turn. Say: Legacy.

55
00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:02.613
Legacy.

56
00:03:03.350 --> 00:03:04.293
Kinship.

57
00:03:05.283 --> 00:03:08.606
Kinship means a family relationship or close connection.

58
00:03:09.217 --> 00:03:11.797
Kinship is very important in Khmer culture.

59
00:03:12.383 --> 00:03:14.838
Your turn. Say: Kinship.

60
00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:18.562
Kinship.

61
00:03:19.317 --> 00:03:20.213
Roots.

62
00:03:21.217 --> 00:03:23.959
Roots means where your family originally comes from.

63
00:03:24.550 --> 00:03:26.503
I want to learn about my roots.

64
00:03:27.117 --> 00:03:29.955
Your turn. Say: Roots.

65
00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:33.743
Roots.

66
00:03:34.533 --> 00:03:38.347
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

67
00:03:39.183 --> 00:03:41.763
Visal, does your family have a special tradition?

68
00:03:42.850 --> 00:03:48.625
Yes. We have a reunion at our ancestral home every Pchum Ben.

69
00:03:49.717 --> 00:03:51.577
How long have you been doing that?

70
00:03:52.683 --> 00:03:56.343
We have been celebrating this tradition for over thirty years!

71
00:03:57.450 --> 00:03:59.333
Is there a generation gap?

72
00:04:00.450 --> 00:04:05.097
A little, but we respect elders, and the gathering strengthens our kinship.

73
00:04:06.233 --> 00:04:07.629
Part three: grammar.

74
00:04:08.483 --> 00:04:11.702
Grammar focus: Present perfect continuous.

75
00:04:12.002 --> 00:04:13.235
Look at the pattern.

76
00:04:13.950 --> 00:04:16.333
We have been celebrating this tradition for years.

77
00:04:17.217 --> 00:04:19.297
My aunt has been cooking since morning.

78
00:04:20.183 --> 00:04:22.009
How long have you been living here?

79
00:04:22.967 --> 00:04:26.893
Use it for actions that started in the past and are still continuing now.

80
00:04:27.650 --> 00:04:31.475
Be careful! Don't say: We have been celebrate for years.

81
00:04:33.433 --> 00:04:36.013
Say: We have been celebrating for years.

82
00:04:36.313 --> 00:04:38.464
After 'have been', use the -ing form.

83
00:04:39.267 --> 00:04:42.012
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

84
00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:44.277
Our Family Reunion.

85
00:04:44.577 --> 00:04:48.144
Every year, our family reunion takes place at our ancestral home.

86
00:04:49.133 --> 00:04:52.584
My grandparents, uncles, aunts and in-laws all come together.

87
00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:56.761
We have been celebrating this family tradition for years.

88
00:04:57.767 --> 00:05:01.775
Despite the generation gap, we honour our heritage and respect elders.

89
00:05:02.767 --> 00:05:06.101
These gatherings keep our roots, kinship and legacy strong.

90
00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:10.981
Part five: quiz. Three questions. Are you ready?

91
00:05:11.817 --> 00:05:14.025
We have blank celebrating for years.

92
00:05:14.325 --> 00:05:17.015
A, been. B, be. C, being.

93
00:05:20.567 --> 00:05:22.322
The answer is A: been.

94
00:05:23.483 --> 00:05:25.994
A meeting after a long time apart is a blank.

95
00:05:26.294 --> 00:05:30.203
A, reunion. B, legacy. C, heritage.

96
00:05:33.767 --> 00:05:35.975
The answer is A: reunion.

97
00:05:37.117 --> 00:05:39.848
Something left for future generations is a blank.

98
00:05:40.148 --> 00:05:43.406
A, legacy. B, roots. C, bond.

99
00:05:46.967 --> 00:05:48.943
The answer is A: legacy.

100
00:05:50.133 --> 00:05:53.691
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

101
00:05:54.550 --> 00:05:57.583
Describe a family tradition that is important to you.

102
00:06:06.383 --> 00:06:07.814
Now listen to Visal.

103
00:06:08.114 --> 00:06:17.454
Every Khmer New Year, we visit my grandmother's village. We have been doing this since I was born, and it keeps our family close.

104
00:06:17.754 --> 00:06:19.776
Excellent answer, Visal!

105
00:06:20.683 --> 00:06:25.611
Great work! Now you can use twelve new words and present perfect continuous.

106
00:06:25.911 --> 00:06:29.481
Today's value is family. Love and help your family.

107
00:06:29.781 --> 00:06:32.178
See you in Unit four. Goodbye!

108
00:06:32.478 --> 00:06:34.933
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

